[32m+[m[32m            "result": "Web search results for query: \"Bliss Lømo 1973 long-term potentiation discovery hippocampus NMDA receptor mechanisms review\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The locus of expression of NMDA receptor-dependent LTP in the | 4 | Co\",\"url\":\"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/edit/10.1201/9781003077046-4/locus-expression-nmda-receptor-dependent-ltp-bliss-fazeli?context=ubx\"},{\"title\":\"Are there common neural mechanisms for learning, epilepsy, and Alzheimer's disease? (Chapter 12) - Neural Networks and Psychopathology\",\"url\":\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/neural-networks-and-psychopathology/are-there-common-neural-mechanisms-for-learning-epilepsy-and-alzheimers-disease/6BFA8149CAC6CD42472BF5889A9AEE09\"},{\"title\":\"Long-term potentiation: peeling the onion\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3718856/\"},{\"title\":\"NMDA receptor-dependent long-term potentiation comprises a family of temporally overlapping forms of synaptic plasticity that are induced by different patterns of stimulation\",\"url\":\"https://www.scienceopen.com/document?vid=042e0688-b69b-44cc-baea-2da4a4fd1d45\"},{\"title\":\"MITECS: Long-Term Potentiation\",\"url\":\"https://people.biology.ucsd.edu/bshanks/hosting/work/papers/symSysHonorsThesis/talkSlides/MITECSLTP.html\"},{\"title\":\"Synaptic plasticity\",\"url\":\"https://webarchiveweb.wayback.bac-lac.canada.ca/web/20160701002438/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synaptic_plasticity\"},{\"title\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://pure.port.ac.uk/ws/files/18175851/The_historial_significance.pdf#3#1\"},{\"title\":\"Discovering long-term potentiation (LTP) - recollections and reflections on what came after - PubMed\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28719040/?dopt=Abstract\"},{\"title\":\"Search CORE\",\"url\":\"https://core.ac.uk/search/?q=Stimulus%20information%20stored%20in%20lasting%20active%20and%20hidden%20network%20states%20is%20destroyed%20by%20network%20bursts\"},{\"title\":\"Multiple cellular cascades participate in long-term potentiation and in hippocampus-dependent learning\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0006899314016175?via%3Dihub\"}]\n\n# Bliss & Lømo's 1973 Discovery of Long-Term Potentiation: Mechanisms and NMDA Receptor Involvement\n\n## The 1973 Discovery\n\nIn 1973, Tim Bliss and Terje Lømo published their landmark discovery of long-term potentiation (LTP) in the *Journal of Physiology*, describing the phenomenon in the dentate area of the hippocampal formation of anesthetized rabbits. They showed that brief, high-frequency stimulation (e.g., 100/sec for 3–4 sec, or 10–20/sec for 10–15 sec) of the perforant path produced a long-lasting increase in synaptic efficacy at perforant path–granule cell synapses, with potentiation lasting from 30 minutes to 10 hours. The original 1973 paper analyzed three parameters of the population response—the population EPSP, and the amplitude and latency of the population spike—and suggested that two independent mechanisms might be responsible: an increase in the efficiency of synaptic transmission at perforant path synapses, and an increase in granule cell excitability. The authors speculated that the effect \"could take the form of... an increase in the amount of transmitter released per synapse, an increase in the sensitivity of the postsynaptic junctional membrane, or a reduction in the resistance of the narrow stem by which spines are attached to the parent dendrite\" (Bliss & Fazeli, via Taylor & Francis).\n\nLømo had first observed the phenomenon in 1966 in Per Andersen's Oslo laboratory, and the experiments with Bliss were conducted in 1968–1969. Interestingly, Lømo later reported that they initially failed to reproduce the results, which led both researchers to leave LTP research for a time (Lømo, PubMed abstract).\n\n## The NMDA Receptor Mechanism\n\nA major subsequent development was the discovery (Collingridge et al., 1983) that the NMDA subtype of glutamate receptor is required for hippocampal LTP. The NMDA receptor (NMDAR) mechanism is now understood as follows:\n\n- **Induction requirements**: Only two requirements are necessary for LTP induction: glutamate binding to the NMDAR and membrane depolarization. Strong postsynaptic depolarization displaces the magnesium ions that block NMDA channels, allowing calcium ions to enter the postsynaptic cell (Wikipedia/Synaptic plasticity; PMC review).\n- **Calcium influx and CaMKII**: Calcium influx through the NMDAR is required for LTP, and CaMKII is the consensus downstream target—both necessary and sufficient. CaMKII activation leads to rapid recruitment of AMPA receptors to the synapse, independent of AMPA receptor subunit type but requiring an adequate pool of surface receptors (PMC review \"Long-term potentiation: peeling the onion\").\n- **Silent synapses**: A key insight was the discovery that some CA1 synapses are postsynaptically silent—containing only functional NMDARs but no AMPA receptors. During LTP, these synapses rapidly acquire AMPA receptor responses, providing a postsynaptic explanation for the observed decrease in synaptic failure rate (Isaac et al., 1995; Liao et al., 1995, cited in PMC review).\n\n## The Locus of Expression Debate\n\nA long-standing controversy concerned whether LTP expression is presynaptic (increased transmitter release) or postsynaptic (increased AMPA receptor response). Evidence for decreased synaptic failure rates initially swayed opinion toward a presynaptic mechanism, but the discovery of silent synapses shifted consensus back to a postsynaptic mechanism. Two-photon microscopy experiments pairing glutamate uncaging onto single spines with postsynaptic depolarization provided unequivocal evidence of a rapid, synapse-specific postsynaptic enhancement of the AMPA receptor response, accompanied by a rapid increase in spine volume—a robust morphological correlate of plasticity. These experiments did not exclude a presynaptic component, but the consensus is that postsynaptic AMPA receptor trafficking is central, with the possibility that the proportion of pre- and postsynaptic contributions changes over time (Bliss & Fazeli; PMC review).\n\n## Multiple Forms and Signaling Cascades\n\nLTP is not a single phenomenon. NMDA receptor-dependent LTP occurs at hippocampal synapses including perforant path–granule cell synapses in the dentate gyrus and Schaffer collateral–CA1 synapses, while mossy fiber LTP in CA3 is NMDA-independent and mediated by presynaptic changes. Reviews emphasize that NMDA-dependent LTP comprises a family of temporally overlapping forms induced by different stimulation patterns (e.g., 100 Hz vs. theta-burst stimulation), and that multiple signaling cascades—including calpain-1/calpain-2, CaMKII, PKA, and ERK—are activated by different stimulus parameters but converge on common mechanisms: regulation of AMPA receptor trafficking and actin-based spine structural changes (ScienceDirect/Brain Research review). The minimal core mechanism is often summarized as: NMDA receptor activation → CaMKII activation → rapid AMPA receptor recruitment to the synapse (PMC review).\n\n## Significance\n\nLTP has remained the most compelling cellular model for learning and memory—the experimental evidence supporting Hebb's 1949 postulate that synaptic strengthening underlies memory formation. Its discovery built on prior work identifying the hippocampus as critical for memory (notably Scoville and Milner's 1957 findings on patient H.M.), and LTP research has since linked NMDA receptor-dependent plasticity to hippocampus-dependent learning, with NMDA antagonists impairing spatial memory tasks such as the Morris water maze. The 1973 discovery is widely regarded as a profound landmark in the history of neuroscience (Pathis, American Journal of Psychology; MITECS entry).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."[m
