[32m+[m[32m            "result": "Web search results for query: \"Sorrells 2018 Nature human hippocampal neurogenesis declines adulthood controversy Eriksson 1998\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"News: Study Finds No Neurogenesis in Adult... (The Scientist) - Behind the headlines\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/search/research-news/5920/\"},{\"title\":\"【期刊论文】 Adult Human Hippocampal Neurogenesis: Controversy and Evidence NSTL 国家科技图书文献中心\",\"url\":\"https://ty.nstl.gov.cn/paper_detail.html?doi=10.1016/j.molmed.2018.04.002\"},{\"title\":\"Neurogenesis in neurodegenerative diseases in the adult human brain\",\"url\":\"https://pjp.termedia.pl/Neurogenesis-in-neurodegenerative-diseases-in-the-adult-human-brain,116,45928,1,1.html\"},{\"title\":\"News: Sorry, Adults, No New Neurons For Your... (NPR News) - Behind the headlines\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.xyz/search/research-news/1588/\"},{\"title\":\"News: New Tools in the Works to Probe Adult... (The Scientist) - Behind the headlines\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.xyz/search/research-news/6126/\"},{\"title\":\"Neurogenèse adulte hippocampique chez l'homme : mythe ou réalité ? - Bordeaux Neurocampus\",\"url\":\"https://www.bordeaux-neurocampus.fr/neurogenese-adulte-hippocampique-chez-lhomme-mythe-ou-realite/\"},{\"title\":\"Human adult neurogenesis: the mystery unveiled - Bordeaux Neurocampus\",\"url\":\"https://www.bordeaux-neurocampus.fr/human-adult-neurogenesis-the-mystery-unveiled/\"},{\"title\":\"The Future of Neurogenesis Therapy\",\"url\":\"https://neurogenesisproject.com/blog/future-of-neurogenesis-therapy\"},{\"title\":\"No Evidence For New Adult Neurons\",\"url\":\"https://www.americanscientist.org/article/no-evidence-for-new-adult-neurons\"},{\"title\":\"Adult neurogenesis in the mammalian dentate gyrus\",\"url\":\"/hdd/c0710/deepseek/datasets/sci-hub/77500000/libgen.scimag77542000-77542999.zip%20---%2010.1111/ahe.12496.pdf#7%234\"}]\n\n## Summary: The 2018 Sorrells Challenge to Eriksson's 1998 Finding on Adult Human Hippocampal Neurogenesis\n\n**Background — Eriksson et al. (1998):** Peter Eriksson and colleagues published \"Neurogenesis in the adult human hippocampus\" in *Nature Medicine* in 1998, providing the first demonstration that new neurons are generated in the adult human hippocampus. Using BrdU labeling, they showed transcriptionally active cells in the dentate gyrus (DG) and subventricular zone (SVZ). This landmark finding overturned the long-held dogma (originally attributed to Ramón y Cajal) that the adult brain is fixed and immutable, and established adult hippocampal neurogenesis as a widely accepted concept.\n\n**The 2018 challenge — Sorrells et al. (Nature, March 7, 2018):** Shawn Sorrells, Arturo Alvarez-Buylla, Mercedes Paredes and colleagues published \"Human hippocampal neurogenesis drops sharply in children to undetectable levels in adults.\" Using 59 human brains, they examined multiple protein markers of young neurons, performed high-resolution light and electron microscopy, analyzed gene expression patterns, and looked for dividing neural stem cells. They found abundant new neurons in fetal brains, a sharp decline during childhood, few remaining by age 13, and none detectable by ages 18–19. They concluded that new neurons do not grow (or are extremely rare) in the adult human hippocampus. They also studied macaque monkeys and reported a developmental difference: in monkeys, neural stem cells organize into a ribbon-like layer, but this organization is absent in humans.\n\n**The immediate controversy:** The paper sparked intense debate because it contradicted ~20 years of research. Weeks later, Boldrini et al. (2018) published \"Human Hippocampal Neurogenesis Persists throughout Aging,\" reporting the opposite conclusion — that neurogenesis persists into old age (individuals up to 80 years), though with declining neural stem cell pools.\n\n**Methodological criticisms of the Sorrells study:** Critics noted several potential problems:\n- **Post-mortem delay:** Sorrells allowed up to 48 hours before tissue fixation, whereas Boldrini limited this to under 26 hours. The DCX (doublecortin) protein — a key marker of immature neurons — can degrade rapidly after death.\n- **Fixation duration:** Prolonged fixation can mask antigens and prevent antibody detection.\n- **Marker reliability:** DCX/PSA-NCAM markers can produce false negatives, and Sorrells reported that DCX can also appear in non-neuronal glial cells.\n- **Subject data:** No medical histories or lifestyle information was provided for the donors, and some died of unknown causes — factors like depression, stress, or medication could affect neurogenesis.\n- **Sample size:** Some key time points had only one individual per group, limiting statistical analysis.\n\n**Sorrells' defense:** The authors defended their findings by noting they used multiple techniques (not just a single marker), confirmed their methods could detect young neurons in fetal tissue, examined rapidly preserved surgical epilepsy tissue, and studied brains collected by multiple collaborators worldwide — all with the same result.\n\n**Later resolution — Moreno-Jiménez et al. (2019):** A subsequent study using optimized tissue processing (post-mortem delay of 3–23 hours, fixation limited to ~24 hours, antigen retrieval, and stereology) identified thousands of DCX+ immature neurons in the dentate gyrus of neurologically healthy humans up to age 87, confirming that adult hippocampal neurogenesis is real. The authors showed that DCX detection drops sharply with prolonged fixation but can be rescued with antigen retrieval — potentially explaining Sorrells' negative results. They also found neurogenesis is sharply impaired in Alzheimer's disease.\n\n**Emerging consensus:** The controversy revealed that the truth is more nuanced than either extreme. Adult human hippocampal neurogenesis likely does continue throughout life, but at a much lower rate than in young animals or rodents, and it varies dramatically based on individual factors such as lifestyle, metabolic state, exercise, sleep, neuroinflammation, and medication exposure. The debate highlighted the need for better tissue-processing methods, more controlled human studies, non-invasive imaging techniques, and single-cell transcriptomic approaches to resolve the question definitively.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."[m
