[32m+[m[32m            "result": "Web search results for query: \"perineuronal nets parvalbumin interneurons myelin plasticity BDNF adult brain plasticity review Nature Reviews Neuroscience\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Non-Cell-Autonomous Factors Implicated in Parvalbumin Interneuron Maturation and Critical Periods\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9115720/?report=classic\"},{\"title\":\"Critical period plasticity in local cortical circuits - Nature Reviews Neuroscience\",\"url\":\"http://nnp.nnchan.ru/glype/browse.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fnrn1787&b=14\"},{\"title\":\"Perineuronal Nets : Neuroscientist\",\"url\":\"https://www.ovid.com/journals/nscie/abstract/10.1177/10738584221106346~perineuronal-nets-subtle-structures-with-large-implications?redirectionsource=fulltextview\"},{\"title\":\"Perineuronal Nets in Neuropsychiatric Disorders\",\"url\":\"https://sage.cnpereading.com/doi/10.26599/BSA.2026.905017\"},{\"title\":\"Parvalbumin-Positive Interneurons Regulate Cortical Sensory Plasticity in Adulthood and Development Through Shared Mechanisms\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9120417/\"},{\"title\":\"Role of Perineuronal Nets in Neuronal Plasticity and Repair: Molecular Perspectives on Psychiatric Disorders\",\"url\":\"https://www.eurekaselect.com/article/155739\"},{\"title\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/9b7f/0539585fec139f343104e1df20de4100496c.pdf#9#1\"},{\"title\":\"Perineuronal Nets: Subtle Structures with Large Implications - PubMed\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35872660/\"},{\"title\":\"Parvalbumin-Positive Interneurons Regulate Cortical Sensory Plasticity in Adulthood and Development Through Shared Mechanisms - Peeref\",\"url\":\"https://www.peeref.com/works/24884863\"}]\n\n# Perineuronal Nets, Parvalbumin Interneurons, and Brain Plasticity: Summary of Key Findings\n\n## Parvalbumin Interneurons and Perineuronal Nets\n\nParvalbumin (PV)-positive interneurons are the largest class of GABAergic inhibitory neurons in the CNS. These fast-spiking cells provide feedforward and feedback inhibition onto pyramidal cells and other interneurons, and their maturation is the critical trigger for developmental plasticity windows (\"critical periods\") (Frontiers in Neural Circuits, 2022; Nature Reviews Neuroscience, 2005). As PV cells mature, they become enwrapped by **perineuronal nets (PNNs)**—specialized, lattice-like extracellular matrix structures composed of a hyaluronan backbone, chondroitin sulfate proteoglycans (aggrecan, neurocan, brevican), link proteins, and tenascin-R, which surround the soma and proximal dendrites of these neurons (Carceller et al., *The Neuroscientist*, 2023; Li et al., 2024).\n\n## Role in Critical Period and Adult Plasticity\n\nPNN appearance coincides with the closure of critical periods across brain regions. PNNs act as \"molecular brakes\" that progressively reduce plasticity as they condense around PV cell somata and proximal dendrites (PMC9115720, 2022). The seminal finding is that **enzymatic removal of PNNs (e.g., via chondroitinase) in adulthood reactivates juvenile-like plasticity**—for example, reinstating ocular dominance plasticity in the adult visual cortex (Pizzorusso et al., 2002, as cited across results; Nature Reviews Neuroscience, 2005). PNNs are not static: they remain dynamic in adulthood, fluctuating in response to sensory experience, neural activity, and diurnal rhythms, with matrix metalloproteinases (especially MMP9) and other proteases (tPA, ADAMTS, cathepsin-S) mediating endogenous PNN remodeling (PMC9115720; Frontiers in Neural Circuits, 2022).\n\n## BDNF Signaling\n\n**Brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF)** signaling through its TrkB receptor on PV cells is one of the first-discovered non-cell-autonomous factors driving critical period onset. BDNF promotes PV interneuron maturation and GABAergic circuit development, accelerating both development and plasticity when overexpressed (Nature Reviews Neuroscience, 2005). Notably, PNNs can dampen PV cell sensitivity to BDNF: chondroitin sulfate proteoglycans activate PTPσ receptors, which dephosphorylate TrkB and abolish BDNF-induced plasticity—meaning PV cells become less responsive to BDNF after critical period closure. BDNF is secreted by neurons, astrocytes, microglia, and oligodendrocytes, with microglial BDNF critical for learning-dependent synaptic plasticity (PMC9115720, 2022).\n\n## Myelination and Myelin Plasticity\n\nMyelination is directly implicated in plasticity regulation: oligodendrocyte-provided myelination initially coincides with critical period opening in sensory cortices but ultimately **participates as an important molecular brake for critical period closure** (PMC9115720, 2022). Neuregulin-1 (NRG1)/ErbB3 signaling in oligodendrocytes is essential during a post-weaning critical period for social learning, affecting oligodendrocyte maturation and **lifelong myelination**. The ECM also supports remyelination processes, and PNN disruption in conditions like multiple sclerosis hinders oligodendrocyte progenitor maturation and remyelination (PMC9115720; PNNs in Neuropsychiatric Disorders review, 2026).\n\n## Nature Reviews Neuroscience Review (2005)\n\nThe *Nature Reviews Neuroscience* article \"Critical period plasticity in local cortical circuits\" (volume 6, pages 877–888, 2005) identifies PV-positive large basket cells as the critical period trigger based on seven lines of evidence: (1) their late postnatal development correlates with plasticity onset; (2) BDNF accelerates both; (3) dark-rearing delays both; (4) they make somatic GABAA α1-subunit synapses; (5) their high firing rates determine plasticity rate; (6) their horizontal axons influence ocular dominance column size; and (7) they are preferentially enwrapped by PNNs with age, whose removal in adulthood reactivates plasticity. The review also describes the proteolytic reorganization of the ECM (via tissue-type plasminogen activator, tPA) during the critical period and proposes \"instructive\" and \"permissive\" models for how GABA circuits trigger plasticity.\n\n## Clinical and Translational Relevance\n\nAcross neuropsychiatric and neurodegenerative disorders (schizophrenia, autism, Alzheimer's disease, epilepsy, ALS, substance use disorders), PNN alterations are consistently associated with disrupted PV interneuron function and E/I imbalance. A central paradox emerges: excessive PNN stabilization prematurely restricts plasticity during development, whereas pathological PNN degradation in adulthood destabilizes mature circuits—positioning PNNs as promising therapeutic targets for restoring circuit balance and plasticity (PNNs in Neuropsychiatric Disorders, 2026; Li et al., 2024; Carceller et al., 2023).\n\n## Key Takeaway\n\nThe interconnected system of PV interneurons, PNNs, BDNF signaling, and myelination forms a unified molecular framework governing both developmental critical period plasticity and adult brain plasticity—where PNNs gate the stability–plasticity balance, BDNF drives maturation and plasticity onset, and myelination acts alongside PNNs to consolidate circuits, with all these mechanisms remaining dynamically regulatable in adulthood.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."[m
