[32m+[m[32m            "result": "Web search results for query: \"Merzenich cortical map plasticity homeostatic synaptic scaling \"plasticity brakes\" critical period review\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Adult neuroplasticity employs developmental mechanisms\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchwithnj.com/en/publications/adult-neuroplasticity-employs-developmental-mechanisms\"},{\"title\":\"Mechanisms of Response Homeostasis During Retinocollicular Map Formation\",\"url\":\"https://xuezheku.aminer.cn/archive/mechanisms-of-response-homeostasis-during-retinocollicular-map-formation/55a498d3c91bf3b1cc3ff609\"},{\"title\":\"Changing Brains\",\"url\":\"https://shop.elsevier.com/books/changing-brains/merzenich/978-0-444-63327-9\"}]\n\nBased on the provided search results, here is a summary addressing the query:\n\n## Merzenich, Cortical Map Plasticity, Homeostatic Scaling, \"Plasticity Brakes,\" and Critical Periods\n\n**Merzenich's foundational cortical map plasticity work.** A 2023 review by Mowery and Garraghty (*Frontiers in Systems Neuroscience*, \"Adult neuroplasticity employs developmental mechanisms\") recounts how Merzenich, Kaas, and colleagues used peripheral nerve transections to provide unambiguous evidence of reorganization in primate somatosensory cortex — directly challenging the Hubel and Wiesel critical-period dogma that the adult brain is immutable. These experiments showed plasticity proceeds in at least two stages: one immediate and one more protracted, findings later extended to the thalamus and brainstem. Critically, receptor subunit mapping (AMPA, NMDA, GABAA, GABAB) after peripheral nerve injury revealed that membrane expression levels in adult cortex came to resemble those seen during early critical-period development — suggesting that prolonged sensory deprivation returns adult cells to critical-period-like plastic states, a phenomenon the authors term \"developmental recapitulation.\"\n\n**Homeostatic synaptic scaling and response homeostasis.** A separate review on \"Mechanisms of Response Homeostasis During Retinocollicular Map Formation\" addresses why homeostatic mechanisms are necessary: Hebbian synaptic plasticity is inherently unstable and would produce runaway excitation or depression if unchecked. The review enumerates stabilizing mechanisms, including **synaptic scaling**, sliding-threshold models of plasticity, dynamic regulation of synapse number and strength, and bidirectional control of intrinsic excitability. It describes \"response homeostasis\" — the finding that total synaptic input onto individual superior colliculus neurons is preserved regardless of receptive-field size — and reviews evidence on which cellular mechanisms underlie this stabilization.\n\n**\"Plasticity brakes\" and critical-period review.** The edited volume *Changing Brains: Applying Brain Plasticity to Advance and Recover Human Ability* (Progress in Brain Research, Vol. 207, 2013), edited by Michael Merzenich, Mor Nahum, and Tom van Vleet, directly addresses the \"brakes\" concept. Its Part 1, Chapter 1 — \"Balancing Plasticity/Stability Across Brain Development\" — includes sections on: critical periods as pruning circuits by early experience; critical-period plasticity of excitatory and inhibitory circuits; **\"Critical Period Triggers and Brakes\"**; and critical-period gating. Chapter 3 (\"Cortical Plasticity, Excitatory–Inhibitory Balance, and Sensory Perception\") further ties adult sensory-cortex plasticity to excitatory–inhibitory balance and neuromodulation. Note: the available snippet only lists these chapter section titles, so the detailed content of the \"brakes\" discussion is not accessible from the search results.\n\n**Synthesis.** Across these sources, a consistent picture emerges: Merzenich's work established that adult cortical maps retain plasticity, with the adult brain able to re-enter critical-period-like states under sensory deprivation; homeostatic mechanisms such as synaptic scaling keep Hebbian plasticity from destabilizing circuits during map formation and refinement; and critical-period \"brakes\" (alongside triggers and gating) govern the balance between plasticity and stability across development — a framework reviewed in Merzenich's own edited volume.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."[m
