eptember 30, 2016 (Volume 17, Number 3, pages 103–186), with DOI: 10.1177/1529100616661983 (PMID: 27697851).\n\n**Background:** The review was motivated by conflicting 2014 open letters: one consensus statement from 70+ scientists claimed brain games lack a scientifically grounded basis for improving cognition or preventing decline, while a counter-letter from 133 scientists and practitioners argued the literature was \"replete\" with demonstrations of benefits. The authors sought to resolve this disagreement by applying well-defined best practices to evaluate the evidence.\n\n**Method:** The team reviewed more than 130 published, peer-reviewed intervention studies cited on the websites of leading brain-training companies (as listed on Cognitive Training Data, the site hosting the proponents' open letter)—i.e., the evidence that best supports industry effectiveness claims.\n\n**Key findings:**\n- **Extensive evidence** that brain-training interventions improve performance on the *trained tasks themselves*.\n- **Less evidence** that training improves performance on *closely related* (but not identical) tasks.\n- **Little evidence** that training enhances performance on *distantly related* tasks or improves *everyday cognitive performance*.\n- **Methodological problems:** Many published studies had major shortcomings in design or analysis that precluded definitive conclusions, and **none of the cited studies conformed to all** of the best practices the authors identified as essential (e.g., preregistration, adequate randomization to treatment/comparison groups, sufficient sample sizes, correction for multiple comparisons, and disclosure of conflicts of interest).\n\n**Conclusion:** As the paper states, \"Practicing a cognitive task consistently improves performance on that task and closely related tasks, but the available evidence that such training generalizes to other tasks or to real-world performance is not compelling.\"\n\n**Recommendations:** The authors issued detailed recommendations for scientists, funding agencies, journalists, and policymakers aimed at producing stronger evidence on brain-training efficacy. The paper was widely noted for raising the scientific bar for the field, with researchers such as Johns Hopkins' George Rebok praising it as \"very even-handed.\"\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."[m
