[32m+[m[32m            "result": "Web search results for query: \"Macnamara Hambrick Oswald 2014 deliberate practice meta-analysis 12% variance performance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://hhs.purdue.edu/skill-learning-and-performance-lab/wp-content/uploads/sites/43/2024/08/Hambrick-Macnamara-Campitelli-Ullen-Mosing-2016-book.pdf#59#3\"},{\"title\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://www.psychologicalscience.org/news/releases/becoming-an-expert-takes-more-than-practice.html?pdf=true#1#1\"},{\"title\":\"Becoming an expert takes more than practice\",\"url\":\"https://research.princeton.edu/news/becoming-expert-takes-more-practice\"},{\"title\":\"Becoming an Expert Takes More Than Practice\",\"url\":\"https://www.psychologicalscience.org/news/releases/becoming-an-expert-takes-more-than-practice.html?utm_campaign=expertisepractice&utm_campaign=expertisepractice&utm_medium=eureka&utm_medium=eureka&utm_source=pressrelease&utm_source=pressrelease\"},{\"title\":\"Practice doesn't always make perfect - it depends on the subject\",\"url\":\"https://www.dailymail.com/sciencetech/article-2679055/Practice-DOESNT-make-perfect-Rehearsing-plays-small-boosting-performance.html\"},{\"title\":\"10000 hours, trading, and what really matters\",\"url\":\"https://finance.yahoo.com/news/1000-hours-what-really-matters-153412176.html\"},{\"title\":\"Can Practice Overcome a Lack of Talent?\",\"url\":\"https://www.psychologytoday.com/us/blog/cutting-edge-leadership/201407/can-practice-overcome-lack-talent\"},{\"title\":\"Deliberate Practice and Performance in Music, Games, Sports, Education, and Professions: A Meta-Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://repository.rice.edu/items/90295c5c-0240-4812-9c3b-5cb69a46372b/full\"},{\"title\":\"Zusammenfassung: Deliberate practice and performance in music, games, sports, education, and professions: a meta-analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.bisp-surf.de/Record/PU201604002392\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict of interest statement\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2014.00751/xml\"}]\n\n## Summary: Macnamara, Hambrick, and Oswald (2014) Meta-Analysis on Deliberate Practice\n\n**The study.** In a meta-analysis published in *Psychological Science* (2014), Brooke N. Macnamara, David Z. Hambrick, and Frederick L. Oswald examined the relationship between deliberate practice and performance across all major domains in which it had been studied (games, music, sports, education, and professions). After searching over 9,300 documents, they identified 88 studies that met their inclusion criteria (studies had to include a measure of activities interpretable as deliberate practice—structured activities created specifically to improve performance—and a performance measure), yielding 157 effect sizes and a total sample size of over 11,000 (11,135) participants.\n\n**The headline finding: 12% of variance explained.** Overall, deliberate practice accounted for only **12% of the variance in performance**, leaving 88% unexplained. Nearly all effect sizes were positive (more practice was associated with higher performance), but the results showed deliberate practice left more variance unexplained than it explained. Even after correcting average effect sizes for unreliability across a wide range of reliability assumptions, deliberate practice still explained well less than half of the variance in performance.\n\n**Domain-specific results.** The effect of deliberate practice varied substantially by domain:\n- **Games:** 26% of variance explained\n- **Music:** 21%\n- **Sports:** 18%\n- **Education:** 4%\n- **Professions:** less than 1% (not statistically significant)\n\nThe authors suggested the weaker effects in education and professions may be because deliberate practice is less well defined in those domains and/or because participants differed in prior expertise.\n\n**Measurement method mattered.** Moderator analyses showed that how practice was measured affected results: deliberate practice explained 20% of variance in studies using retrospective interviews, 12% for retrospective questionnaires, but only 5% for log methods (ongoing recording of activity). Because the log method presumably yields more valid estimates, the authors noted the true relationship may be even weaker than the meta-analysis indicated.\n\n**Conclusion.** The authors concluded that deliberate practice is important—\"unquestionably important\" from both statistical and theoretical perspectives—but \"not nearly as important as proponents of the view have claimed\" (e.g., the popular \"10,000-hour rule\"). They argued that other factors, such as starting age, working memory capacity, and cognitive abilities, likely account for much of the remaining variance in performance.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."[m
