[32m+[m[32m            "result": "Web search results for query: \"Jaeggi 2008 working memory training fluid intelligence N-back replication failure\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"\",\"url\":\"https://pure.mpg.de/rest/items/item_3404363_1/component/file_3429588/content#14#11\"},{\"title\":\"Revision 58795f75-45c5-4d6c-a68b-0ed9d8d378cf\",\"url\":\"https://psychology.stackexchange.com/revisions/58795f75-45c5-4d6c-a68b-0ed9d8d378cf/view-source\"},{\"title\":\"Revision b438f4df-f8d9-4923-9eae-2a86f70c28ad\",\"url\":\"https://psychology.stackexchange.com/revisions/b438f4df-f8d9-4923-9eae-2a86f70c28ad/view-source\"},{\"title\":\"Library Search\",\"url\":\"https://librarysearch.temple.edu/articles?f%5Btopic%5D%5B%5D=Psychology&q=Control+Groups&search_field=subject&sort=date\"},{\"title\":\"Computer- and Suggestion-based Cognitive Rehabilitation following Acquired Brain Injury.; Computer- og suggestionsbaseret kognitiv rehabilitering efter erhvervet hjerneskade.\",\"url\":\"/hf3fs-jd/hdd/deepseek/shared/guankangagi/oatd_pdfs/26/2785826.pdf#25#5\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Improving fluid intelligence with training on working memory | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Improving-fluid-intelligence-with-training-on-Jaeggi-Buschkuehl/2e776c3af634ab4ff40a67e6295efcf3a3879848\"},{\"title\":\"Working memory training does not improve intelligence in healthy young adults\",\"url\":\"https://librarysearch.temple.edu/articles/cdi_proquest_miscellaneous_1627977386\"}]\n\n## Summary: Jaeggi et al. (2008), Working Memory Training, Fluid Intelligence, and Replication Failures\n\n### The Original Claim (Jaeggi et al., 2008)\nSusanne Jaeggi and colleagues published a landmark paper in the *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences* claiming that training on a **dual n-back working memory task** over several weeks produced **transfer effects to fluid intelligence (Gf)**, as measured by tests like Raven's Progressive Matrices and the Bochumer Matrices Test (BOMAT). This finding generated considerable \"hype\" about brain training. Jaeggi et al. also proposed a **dosage-response claim** — that more training sessions produced greater gains in fluid intelligence.\n\n### Replication Failures\nMultiple independent attempts to replicate the finding failed:\n\n- **Redick et al. (2012/2013)**: Sought to replicate Jaeggi et al.'s methods with methodological extensions — stratifying 140 subjects into three groups, testing them on 17 different tests at three timepoints (75 completed, a 43% dropout rate). Although subjects improved on the training task itself, there was **no differential improvement on any outcome measure**, discrediting both the transfer claim and the dosage-response claim.\n\n- **Chooi & Thompson (2012)**: An independent, high-quality N-back study with 130 starting and 93 completing participants assigned to passive control, active control, and experimental groups, across 8-day and 20-day training conditions. Despite improved training-task performance, there was **no significant improvement in fluid intelligence or working memory capacity**, and no improvement in verbal, perceptual, or spatial abilities.\n\n- **Thompson et al. (2013)**: Also found no transfer.\n\n- **Other null results**: Later replications by Jaeggi's own group (Jaeggi, Buschkuehl, Jonides & Shah, 2011) and others yielded null results. Most subsequent studies could not detect maintained improvements in working memory or fluid intelligence, nor generalization to other skill domains.\n\n### Methodological Critiques of the 2008 Study\n- **Data lumping**: Redick et al. uncovered that Jaeggi et al. (2008) had **lumped together several very small experiments using different outcome measures** and reported them as if they were the same experiment with the same outcome measure — a serious methodological flaw.\n- **Control group issues**: Some studies were criticized for lacking active control groups, raising the possibility that effects reflected a \"Hawthorne effect.\" Where active controls were used, the control tasks were sometimes criticized for differing in engagement or challenge.\n- **Measurement issues**: Shipstead et al. (2012) noted that many studies used only a single test per cognitive construct, so effects could be test-specific rather than reflecting genuine transfer.\n\n### Proposed Explanations for the Failed Replication\n- **Participant payment and motivation**: Jaeggi et al. (2014) noted that transfer was found only in studies where participants were unpaid or paid very little (e.g., $20); studies paying more ($130–$800) found no transfer. Paid participants also showed flatter training curves. Extrinsic rewards may have undermined intrinsic motivation.\n- **Ceiling effects**: Thompson et al. suggested that above-average participant IQ (mean ~121) may have created a ceiling effect preventing detection of improvement.\n- **Task-specific strategies**: Participants may develop task-specific strategies that improve n-back performance without transferring to other tasks. Notably, individuals with higher baseline IQ improved more on the n-back task itself but showed no greater transfer.\n- **Training duration/settings**: Longer training may lead to strategy development or motivation decline; differences in training location (home vs. lab) could also matter.\n- **Training dose**: The dissertation study reported that participants trained roughly twice as long as in Jaeggi et al. (2008) yet still showed no transfer, contradicting the dosage-response claim.\n\n### Meta-Analytic Evidence\nThe meta-analysis by **Melby-Lervåg & Hulme (2012)** found moderate effect sizes for **near-transfer** tasks (similar to the trained task) but **no transfer to untrained constructs** such as intelligence. Reviews by Shipstead, Redick, & Engle (2012) concluded that effects were too inconsistent across studies to establish that working memory training improves higher-level cognition. A later multilevel meta-analysis (Sala & Gobet, 2020) concluded there was no evidence for generalized effects of working memory training.\n\n### Overall Conclusion from the Evidence\nThe consensus across the provided sources is that the **Jaeggi et al. (2008) finding of n-back training improving fluid intelligence has failed to replicate** in well-controlled studies. Training reliably improves performance on the trained task itself (and sometimes near-transfer tasks), but there is no convincing evidence that it improves fluid intelligence, working memory capacity, or other untrained cognitive abilities in healthy adults.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."[m
